    "source": "q3_release",
    "note": "Apply an analyst-selected 4% nominal escalation to the modeled 2026 CAS per physical unit for 2027. The source provides a directional anchor that cost inflation is expected to exceed the 3% assumption in the 2026 guidance. Management did not publish a specific 4% rate or mine-level gross unit costs. The assumption is editable."
  },
  "limitations": [
    "Forward gross site cost, on-site grade and throughput schedules, stripping, fuel curves, individual royalty schedules, and payable inventory projections were not disclosed in the 2027 indications.",
    "Filed CAS includes inventory effects, contract and cost allocation practices, and royalties. These cost bases are useful for a dated scenario, with mine-plan, jurisdictional tax and royalty modeling needed for valuation.",
    "Use each physical metal sales denominator. Treat portfolio by-product CAS and AISC as disclosure cross-checks. Separate metal sales are already recognized in GAAP Sales and cannot be deducted again from GAAP operating expense.",
    "Do not treat corporate and other historical CAS of negative $5m as a mine unit cost. Keep any historic adjustment separate and allocate forecast CAS to reportable operating sites.",
    "Keep capex, reclamation cash, and AISC separate. Full-year attributable capital guidance and filed consolidated cash capex are on different bases. Red Chris and Yanacocha plans depend on investment, timing and permit assumptions."
  ]
}
